1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,250
A football sits still until it is kicked.

2
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:05,264
A rolling bicycle slows down.

3
00:00:05,264 --> 00:00:07,449
A loaded cart is hard to push.

4
00:00:07,849 --> 00:00:10,180
Start, stop, speed up, slow down.

5
00:00:10,180 --> 00:00:12,193
What makes motion change at all?

6
00:00:12,643 --> 00:00:15,772
Aarav puts a coin on a
card, on top of a glass.

7
00:00:15,772 --> 00:00:17,656
Then he flicks the card away.

8
00:00:18,006 --> 00:00:19,321
The card flies off.

9
00:00:19,321 --> 00:00:21,950
But the coin drops
straight into the glass.

10
00:00:21,950 --> 00:00:24,122
Why does it not go with the card?

11
00:00:24,622 --> 00:00:25,715
Let's find out.

12
00:00:25,715 --> 00:00:28,615
It is the first idea
in the laws of motion.

13
00:00:29,215 --> 00:00:30,751
First, what is a force?

14
00:00:31,101 --> 00:00:33,066
A force is a push or a pull.

15
00:00:33,466 --> 00:00:37,294
It can start, stop, speed up,
slow down, or turn a motion.

16
00:00:37,294 --> 00:00:39,051
It can even change a shape.

17
00:00:39,451 --> 00:00:41,321
Force is measured in newtons.

18
00:00:41,321 --> 00:00:44,173
It acts in a direction, so it is a vector.

19
00:00:44,823 --> 00:00:49,100
Over two thousand years ago, Aristotle
said a moving body needs a force

20
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:50,427
to keep it moving.

21
00:00:50,827 --> 00:00:51,972
It looks true.

22
00:00:51,972 --> 00:00:54,831
Stop pedalling, and a bicycle slows down.

23
00:00:55,231 --> 00:00:57,582
But Galileo asked a better question.

24
00:00:57,582 --> 00:00:59,289
What is slowing it down?

25
00:00:59,939 --> 00:01:04,254
Galileo rolled a ball down one smooth
ramp, and up a second one facing

26
00:01:04,254 --> 00:01:05,154
it.

27
00:01:05,096 --> 00:01:07,284
It climbs back to almost the same height.

28
00:01:07,684 --> 00:01:09,297
Now a gentler second ramp.

29
00:01:09,297 --> 00:01:12,391
It still climbs to that
height, so it rolls farther.

30
00:01:12,791 --> 00:01:13,868
Flatter still.

31
00:01:13,868 --> 00:01:14,945
Farther again.

32
00:01:16,445 --> 00:01:17,593
Now lay it flat.

33
00:01:17,593 --> 00:01:20,298
The ball can never climb
back to that height.

34
00:01:20,698 --> 00:01:23,896
So it keeps rolling, at
the same speed, on and on.

35
00:01:23,896 --> 00:01:25,535
The speed graph goes flat.

36
00:01:26,185 --> 00:01:27,293
Remember this.

37
00:01:27,293 --> 00:01:29,428
Motion does not need a force.

38
00:01:29,428 --> 00:01:31,241
A change in motion does.

39
00:01:31,941 --> 00:01:33,048
Side by side.

40
00:01:33,048 --> 00:01:36,637
Aristotle: a force is needed
to keep a body moving.

41
00:01:37,037 --> 00:01:40,239
Galileo: a force is needed
only to change motion.

42
00:01:40,239 --> 00:01:43,193
Things stop because
friction and air push back.

43
00:01:43,793 --> 00:01:44,843
Now you try.

44
00:01:44,843 --> 00:01:49,319
A ball rolls at the same speed
on a carpet, and on smooth tiles.

45
00:01:49,319 --> 00:01:50,726
Which goes farther?

46
00:01:50,726 --> 00:01:52,031
Pause and decide.

47
00:01:55,231 --> 00:01:56,131
The tiles!

48
00:01:56,121 --> 00:02:00,909
Less friction means a smaller force
against the ball, so its motion changes

49
00:02:00,909 --> 00:02:01,902
more slowly.

50
00:02:02,552 --> 00:02:04,576
Every object has this property.

51
00:02:04,576 --> 00:02:08,190
It is called inertia, and it
means resistance to change.

52
00:02:08,590 --> 00:02:11,317
At rest, an object tends to stay at rest.

53
00:02:11,317 --> 00:02:14,230
Moving, it tends to keep
moving the same way.

54
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:16,260
Back to Aarav's coin.

55
00:02:16,260 --> 00:02:20,455
The flick pushes only the card,
which shoots away in a split second.

56
00:02:20,855 --> 00:02:21,953
Slow-mo replay.

57
00:02:21,953 --> 00:02:24,007
Freeze, and draw the force.

58
00:02:24,407 --> 00:02:27,709
The orange push acts on
the card, not on the coin.

59
00:02:27,709 --> 00:02:32,309
Only a tiny friction drags the coin,
for about two hundredths of a second.

60
00:02:32,709 --> 00:02:35,246
So the coin moves less than a millimetre.

61
00:02:35,246 --> 00:02:37,783
On the graph, the card's line shoots up.

62
00:02:37,783 --> 00:02:39,563
The coin's stays almost flat.

63
00:02:39,963 --> 00:02:42,048
Inertia keeps the coin in place.

64
00:02:42,048 --> 00:02:45,200
Then gravity pulls it
straight down into the glass.

65
00:02:45,850 --> 00:02:48,021
Is inertia the same for everything?

66
00:02:48,021 --> 00:02:50,440
Let's test two carts on a smooth floor.

67
00:02:50,790 --> 00:02:54,721
An empty cart of ten kilograms,
and a loaded one of forty.

68
00:02:55,121 --> 00:02:57,951
Push each one with twenty
newtons, for one second.

69
00:02:58,351 --> 00:03:01,316
The empty cart reaches
two metres per second.

70
00:03:01,316 --> 00:03:03,971
The loaded cart, only zero point five.

71
00:03:04,371 --> 00:03:05,977
Same push, same time.

72
00:03:05,977 --> 00:03:09,706
Four times the mass, one
quarter of the change in speed.

73
00:03:10,356 --> 00:03:12,243
Now the rule, in three steps.

74
00:03:12,593 --> 00:03:13,562
Step one.

75
00:03:13,562 --> 00:03:15,440
No force, no change.

76
00:03:15,440 --> 00:03:16,963
Delta v is zero.

77
00:03:17,713 --> 00:03:18,676
Step two.

78
00:03:18,676 --> 00:03:20,673
A force means a change.

79
00:03:20,673 --> 00:03:22,494
Delta v is not zero.

80
00:03:23,244 --> 00:03:24,233
Step three.

81
00:03:24,233 --> 00:03:29,198
Same force, same time: delta v is
inversely proportional to the mass.

82
00:03:29,948 --> 00:03:31,240
Now link it up.

83
00:03:31,240 --> 00:03:34,347
More mass gives a smaller
change in velocity.

84
00:03:34,747 --> 00:03:38,586
And a smaller change means a
stronger resistance to change.

85
00:03:38,986 --> 00:03:41,517
And resistance to change is inertia.

86
00:03:41,517 --> 00:03:44,005
So mass is the measure of inertia.

87
00:03:44,655 --> 00:03:45,555
Your turn.

88
00:03:45,546 --> 00:03:48,393
Both carts roll at two metres per second.

89
00:03:48,393 --> 00:03:50,451
Which one is harder to stop?

90
00:03:50,451 --> 00:03:51,762
Pause and decide.

91
00:03:54,962 --> 00:03:56,170
The loaded cart!

92
00:03:56,170 --> 00:04:00,602
More mass, more inertia, and inertia
resists every change, starting or

93
00:04:00,602 --> 00:04:01,502
stopping.

94
00:04:01,999 --> 00:04:03,734
Now, one worked example.

95
00:04:04,084 --> 00:04:09,051
Same push, same time: a two kilogram
trolley gains three metres per second.

96
00:04:09,051 --> 00:04:11,615
What does a six kilogram trolley gain?

97
00:04:12,115 --> 00:04:15,433
Delta v is inversely proportional to mass.

98
00:04:15,433 --> 00:04:20,094
So delta v two over delta v
one equals m one over m two.

99
00:04:20,844 --> 00:04:23,452
That is two over six, which is one third.

100
00:04:24,202 --> 00:04:26,902
So delta v two is one metre per second.

101
00:04:26,902 --> 00:04:30,046
Three times the mass,
one third of the change.

102
00:04:30,696 --> 00:04:32,161
Now, the exam trap.

103
00:04:32,511 --> 00:04:35,926
Many students say a faster
object has more inertia.

104
00:04:36,326 --> 00:04:38,681
But inertia depends only on mass.

105
00:04:38,681 --> 00:04:43,525
A one kilogram ball has the same
inertia at rest, or at fifty metres per

106
00:04:43,525 --> 00:04:44,425
second.

107
00:04:44,862 --> 00:04:46,375
Where is this asked?

108
00:04:46,375 --> 00:04:50,072
In concept questions on
inertia, and on Galileo's ramps.

109
00:04:50,672 --> 00:04:51,904
Exam level now.

110
00:04:51,904 --> 00:04:56,654
A ball rolls down a smooth ramp, and
up a second smooth ramp at thirty

111
00:04:56,654 --> 00:04:57,554
degrees.

112
00:04:57,395 --> 00:05:00,182
That ramp is lowered to fifteen degrees.

113
00:05:00,182 --> 00:05:01,247
What happens?

114
00:05:01,247 --> 00:05:02,531
Pause and solve.

115
00:05:07,931 --> 00:05:09,292
The answer is A.

116
00:05:09,792 --> 00:05:14,188
With no energy lost, the ball climbs
back to its starting height, h.

117
00:05:14,688 --> 00:05:18,351
The distance along a ramp
is h divided by sine theta.

118
00:05:18,851 --> 00:05:21,813
So the ratio is sine
thirty over sine fifteen.

119
00:05:21,813 --> 00:05:24,775
That is about one point
nine three, not two.

120
00:05:25,375 --> 00:05:26,834
Here is your recap.

121
00:05:26,834 --> 00:05:28,334
Screenshot this card.

122
00:05:31,534 --> 00:05:33,464
Can you answer these without help?

123
00:05:33,864 --> 00:05:34,764
One.

124
00:05:34,356 --> 00:05:38,388
Why does the coin drop into the
glass when the card is flicked?

125
00:05:39,288 --> 00:05:40,188
Two.

126
00:05:39,821 --> 00:05:43,653
Why is a loaded cart harder
to stop than an empty one?

127
00:05:44,553 --> 00:05:45,453
Three.

128
00:05:45,260 --> 00:05:49,365
What did Galileo's flat ramp
show about Aristotle's idea?

129
00:05:50,465 --> 00:05:54,994
Next time: if nothing pushes a moving
ball, does it really never stop?

130
00:05:54,994 --> 00:05:57,216
We find out in Newton's first law.
